---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TextDiffChecker from '@/components/tools/text/TextDiffChecker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="text-diff-checker">
  <TextDiffChecker client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to compare two texts</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Put the original version in the left box and the newer version in the right box. You can drop files directly onto either box.</li>
      <li>The comparison runs automatically. Removed lines are tinted and marked with a minus, added lines with a plus, and edited lines have the changed words highlighted.</li>
      <li>Turn on <strong>Ignore whitespace</strong> when only the indentation or wrapping changed, and <strong>Ignore case</strong> when capitalisation does not matter.</li>
      <li>Switch between side-by-side and unified views. Unified is easier on a phone and matches what you see in a code review.</li>
      <li>Copy or download the unified diff to attach to a ticket, an email or a pull request.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How the diff is calculated</h2>
    <p>
      The text diff checker uses Eugene Myers’ 1986 O(ND) greedy algorithm, the same method as
      <code>git diff</code> and GNU diff. It finds the shortest edit script: the smallest set of insertions
      and deletions that turns the first text into the second. So a moved paragraph shows as one deletion and
      one insertion, not as a series of unrelated changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Myers slows down when two large files have little in common. So the tool first trims the identical
      start and end, then splits the remaining work on patience anchors: lines that appear exactly once in
      both texts, such as a unique heading or function signature. Each gap between anchors is small enough
      for Myers to solve at once, and the anchors keep the alignment correct in files with much repeated
      boilerplate.
    </p>
    <p>
      After the lines are aligned, each pair of a deleted and an inserted line is compared again at word
      level with the same algorithm on tokens. This gives the inline highlighting.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the change summary</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Figure</th>
            <th>What it means</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Added</td><td>Lines that exist only in the new text</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Removed</td><td>Lines that exist only in the original</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Modified</td><td>Lines edited in place: a removal and an insertion that the tool paired</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Unchanged</td><td>Lines identical in both, under your current comparison options</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Similarity</td><td>Unchanged lines as a percentage of the longer document</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Contract review: find the clause that changed between two supplier drafts, without a tracked-changes copy.</li>
      <li>Editing: see exactly what an editor changed in a manuscript or a press release.</li>
      <li>Configuration debugging: compare a working config with a broken one when “nothing changed”.</li>
      <li>Translation and localisation: check which strings changed between two export files.</li>
      <li>Plagiarism spot-checks: measure how much two submissions overlap.</li>
      <li>Offline code review: paste two versions of a function and read the diff without a repository.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Unified diff format</h2>
    <p>
      The downloadable file is a standard unified patch. Each hunk starts with a header such as
      <code>@@ -12,7 +12,9 @@</code>, which means “seven lines from line 12 of the original became nine lines
      from line 12 of the new file”. Context lines start with a space, removals with <code>-</code> and
      additions with <code>+</code>. Each change has three lines of context, and nearby changes merge into one
      hunk. <code>git apply</code>, <code>patch</code> and most review platforms accept it directly.
    </p>

    <h2>Privacy and size limits</h2>
    <p>
      Both texts stay in your browser, so you can compare a confidential contract or an unreleased document.
      Files of a few megabytes work well. Above about 6,000 changed lines, the tool skips word-level
      highlighting so the page stays responsive, and the on-screen table shows at most 1,200 rows. The
      downloadable diff always contains every change.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
